WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.020
The safest default is for the main application, user interface, or ordinary service logic to run with ordinary permissions.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:18.440
Low-privilege code can parse input, display UI, prepare requests, validate basic user intent, handle non-sensitive workflow steps, and report errors.

3
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:23.740
It should not gain broad authority simply because a user clicked a button or opened a file.

4
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:28.580
This design reduces the consequences of routine defects.

5
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:38.040
A parsing error, rendering issue, plugin problem, or UI mistake should not automatically become a protected file change or service configuration update.

6
00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:45.020
The low-privilege core should be able to say what it wants done, but the privileged boundary should decide whether the action is allowed.

7
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.260
Low-privilege components still need strong engineering.

8
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:56.520
They should validate input before building requests, preserve clear state, handle cancellation, and avoid collecting secrets they do not need.

9
00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.780
They should also avoid embedding privileged assumptions in presentation code.

10
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.920
A clean request model makes it easier for helpers and brokers to verify intent.

11
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:12.200
When privileged work is required, the low-privilege component should request a narrow operation through a controlled boundary.

12
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:18.940
The request should name the action, the target resource, the caller context, and any validated parameters.

13
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.120
The boundary should then authorize and execute only the approved operation.

14
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.120
Good low-privilege design also improves testing.

15
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:33.100
Teams can exercise most workflows without special rights and reserve focused tests for the small boundary where authority is actually held.

16
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.680
That keeps the architecture understandable and the privileged surface easier to review.
